<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bizen ware</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bizen ware is a Japanese stoneware made around Imbe in the former Bizen Province, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama-prefecture">Okayama Prefecture</a>. One of the Six Old Kilns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, it is fired at high temperature without glaze in wood-burning kilns, which give its reddish-brown surface natural ash and flame markings. Prized for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> wares from the 16th century, it is still produced today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bizen-ware.jpg" alt="Bizen ware" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flower_Vase_called_%27Tabimakura%27_(Portable_pillow),_Bizen_ware,_Edo_period,_17th_century_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_DSC05874.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bizen ware takes its name from the province where it was traditionally made, around the village of Imbe, and is also called Imbe or Inbe ware. It has ties to the older Sue pottery and appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period. The scholar Koyama Fujio counted it among the Six Ancient Kilns. Its peak came in the Momoyama period of the 16th century, and its rustic quality made it popular in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a>; early pieces are called old Bizen style, Ko-Bizen-ha.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the Ikeda lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> domain supported the kilns and gave privileges to the families who ran them, among them the Kimura, Mori, Kaneshige, Oae, Tongu and Terami. After modernisation began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a> the craft almost disappeared, until the potter Kaneshige Toyo (1896–1967) revived the Momoyama style in the 1930s and was named a Living National Treasure. The government designated Bizen ware a traditional Japanese craft in 1982.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The clay of Imbe is sticky and fine, rich in iron and organic matter. It shrinks so much that an applied glaze would peel off in the kiln, and it cannot bear rapid changes of temperature, but its plasticity and strength let pieces keep their form and stay tough without glaze. Most vessels are thrown on a wheel, and their surfaces depend on yohen, the effects of the kiln.</p><p>Firings take place once or twice a year in climbing kilns, noborigama, or tunnel kilns, anagama, and last 10–14 days. Red pine is burned for its resin, and the potter adds wood every 20 minutes, day and night, raising the heat gradually towards about 1200 Celsius. Melted ash gives the yellow speckles called goma; wrapping a piece in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-straw rope soaked in salt water produces the scarlet lines of hidasuki; burying part of a vessel in sand gives the dark sangiri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>At the beginning of the 21st century Bizen ware was made in around 300 kilns, and a Bizen ware festival is held every year around Imbe Station. The Bizen Ware Traditional Industry Hall there shows contemporary work and old pieces, and the Okayama Prefectural Bizen Ceramics Museum holds more than 500.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bizen ware” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
